lechat date  |  | (None)\nL110:  | Responsible AD  |  | (None)\nL111:  | Send notices to  |  | (None)\nL112: Email authors (draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model@ietf.org) 【78†IPR】 【79†References】 【80†Referenced by】 【81†Nits†author-tools.ietf.org】 【82†Nits v3†author-tools.ietf.org】 【83†Search email archive†mailarchive.ietf.org】\nL113: draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model-02\nL114: \nL115:     DAWN                                                              Y. Cui\nL116:     Internet-Draft                                       Tsinghua University\nL117:     Intended status: Informational                                    K. Yao\nL118:     Expires: 6 May 2027                                         China Mobile\nL119:                                                              2 November 2026\nL120: \nL121:     Minimum Public Discovery Information: Requirements and Common Semantics\nL122:                           draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model-02\nL123: \nL124:     Abstract\nL125: \nL126:        The charter of the Discovery of Agents With Names (DAWN) working\nL127:        group asks it to specify the minimum public discovery information\nL128:        that a client obtains when it discovers an AI resource.  An\nL129:        individual draft on DAWN terminology defines Minimum Discoverable\nL130:        Information (MDI) as the minimum amount of information an entity\nL131:        needs to provide to become discoverable, distinguishes it from the\nL132:        Minimum Viable Dataset (MVD) that a discovery mechanism needs as\nL133:        input, and gives only examples of the content of either.  Individual\nL134:        drafts on the DAWN problem statement and requirements call for a\nL135:        standardized minimum structure and list what it must at least\nL136:        contain; in its absence, emerging discovery formats risk each\nL137:        defining its own minimal metadata, with descriptors that do not\nL138:        interoperate across organizational or protocol boundaries.\nL139:        This document addresses the minimum public discovery information as a\nL140:        client obtains it and as a publisher provides it, and its relation to\nL141:        the input of discovery; registration mechanisms are outside its\nL142:        scope.  Using the distinction RFC 3444 draws between information\nL143:        models and data models, it describes the information involved in\nL144:        publication, lookup, and response; proposes a procedure that derives\nL145:        what is minimal for each deployment context from the scope of the\nL146:        charter; applies the procedure to the charter's three deployment\nL147:        contexts: a local network, an organization, and the domain of a\nL148:        cooperating party; and proposes a set of elements with the minimal\nL149:        constraints that hold among them.  Information can be supplied by the\nL150:        discovery response, by context the client already has, or by binding\nL151:        rules.  This document is input to the DAWN architecture and protocol\nL152:        work; normative definitions that affect interoperability belong to\nL153:        the corresponding Standards Track documents.  It is a mapping target\nL154:        for richer descriptors.\nL155: \nL156:     Discussion Venues\nL157: \nL158:        This note is to be removed before publishing as an RFC.\nL159: \nL160:     Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                   [Page 1]\nL161:     Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL162: \nL163:        Discussion of this document takes place on the Discovery of Agents\nL164:        With Names Working Group mailing list (dawn@ietf.org), which is\nL165:        archived at https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/browse/dawn.\nL166: \nL167:        Source for this draft and an issue tracker can be found at\nL168:        https://github.com/nxbrowning/MDI.\nL169: \nL170:     Status of This Memo\nL171: \nL172:        This Internet-Draft is submitted in 