. . . . .  30\nL231: \nL232:     1.  Introduction\nL233: \nL234:        Clients often need to discover information about AI resources, such\nL235:        as AI agents, before interacting with them [DAWN-CHARTER], and often\nL236:        have no pre-configured relationship with them.  Before any\nL237:        interaction can begin, a client needs a small amount of information\nL238:        about the AI resource: what it is, what it does, how to reach it, and\nL239:        what assurances, if any, accompany those claims.\nL240:        [I-D.farrel-dawn-terminology] defines Minimum Discoverable\nL241:        Information (MDI) as the minimum amount of information an entity\nL242:        needs to provide to become discoverable, and does not define its\nL243:        content; [I-D.king-dawn-requirements] requires each discovery\nL244:        mechanism to define the MDI, including at least the entity's type,\nL245:        the services it offers, its reachability information, and the\nL246:        communication protocols and any security parameters needed to\nL247:        interact with it, and to indicate whether discovery information is\nL248:        static or dynamic; [I-D.akhavain-moussa-dawn-problem-statement] calls\nL249:        directly for a standardized structure carrying the minimum\nL250:        information a discovery response returns; and\nL251:        [I-D.moussa-dawn-gap-analysis] sketches a provisional baseline\nL252:        without normalizing it.  Mechanism-specific drafts define such fields\nL253:        for their carriers ([I-D.jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns],\nL254:        [I-D.blwm-dawn-common-discovery-parameters]); none defines MDI\nL255:        content independently of a carrier.\nL256: \nL257:        The DAWN charter [DAWN-CHARTER] places this information at the center\nL258:        of the working group's scope: discovery is limited to a client\nL259:        requesting and obtaining the minimum public discovery information for\nL260:        an AI resource (for example, its type, reachability information,\nL261: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model-02.txt)\nciteturn55view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model-02.txt\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1539\nL0: DAWN                                                              Y. Cui\nL1: Internet-Draft                                       Tsinghua University\nL2: Intended status: Informational                                    K. Yao\nL3: Expires: 6 May 2027                                         China Mobile\nL4:                                                          2 November 2026\nL5: \nL6: \nL7: Minimum Public Discovery Information: Requirements and Common Semantics\nL8:                       draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model-02\nL9: \nL10: Abstract\nL11:    The charter of the Discovery of Agents With Names (DAWN) working\nL12:    group asks it to specify the minimum public discovery information\nL13:    that a client obtains when it discovers an AI resource.  An\nL14:    individual draft on DAWN terminology defines Minimum Discoverable\nL15:    Information (MDI) as the minimum amount of information an entity\nL16:    needs to provide to become discoverable, distinguishes it from the\nL17:    Minimum Viable Dataset (MVD) that a discovery mechanism needs as\nL18:    input, and gives only examples of the content of either.  Individual\nL19:    drafts on the DAWN problem statement and requirements call for a\nL20:    standardized minimum structure and list what it must at least\nL21:    contain; in its absence, emerging discovery formats risk each\nL22:    defining its own minimal metadata, with descriptors that do not\nL23:    interoperate across organizational or protocol boundaries.\nL24:    This document addresses the minimum public discovery informatio