                 Expires 6 May 2027                   [Page 2]\nL98: \nL99: \nL100: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL101: \nL102:    8.  MDI Information Model . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  14\nL103:      8.1.  Structure . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  15\nL104:      8.2.  Elements  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  16\nL105:      8.3.  Information Beyond the Minimum  . . . . . . . . . . . . .  18\nL106:      8.4.  Services  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  19\nL107:      8.5.  Minimal Constraints . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  20\nL108:    9.  Binding Expectations  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  21\nL109:    10. Security Considerations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  22\nL110:    11. Privacy Considerations  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  23\nL111:    12. IANA Considerations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  24\nL112:    13. Open Issues . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  24\nL113:    14. References  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  25\nL114:      14.1.  Normative References . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  25\nL115:      14.2.  Informative References . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  25\nL116:    Appendix A.  Illustrative JSON Example  . . . . . . . . . . . . .  27\nL117:    Appendix B.  Mapping Notes  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  28\nL118:    Acknowledgements  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  30\nL119:    Authors' Addresses  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  30\nL120: 1.  Introduction\nL121:    Clients often need to discover information about AI resources, such\nL122:    as AI agents, before interacting with them [DAWN-CHARTER], and often\nL123:    have no pre-configured relationship with them.  Before any\nL124:    interaction can begin, a client needs a small amount of information\nL125:    about the AI resource: what it is, what it does, how to reach it, and\nL126:    what assurances, if any, accompany those claims.\nL127:    [I-D.farrel-dawn-terminology] defines Minimum Discoverable\nL128:    Information (MDI) as the minimum amount of information an entity\nL129:    needs to provide to become discoverable, and does not define its\nL130:    content; [I-D.king-dawn-requirements] requires each discovery\nL131:    mechanism to define the MDI, including at least the entity's type,\nL132:    the services it offers, its reachability information, and the\nL133:    communication protocols and any security parameters needed to\nL134:    interact with it, and to indicate whether discovery information is\nL135:    static or dynamic; [I-D.akhavain-moussa-dawn-problem-statement] calls\nL136:    directly for a standardized structure carrying the minimum\nL137:    information a discovery response returns; and\nL138:    [I-D.moussa-dawn-gap-analysis] sketches a provisional baseline\nL139:    without normalizing it.  Mechanism-specific drafts define such fields\nL140:    for their carriers ([I-D.jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns],\nL141:    [I-D.blwm-dawn-common-discovery-parameters]); none defines MDI\nL142:    content independently of a carrier.\nL143:    The DAWN charter [DAWN-CHARTER] places this information at the center\nL144:    of the working group's scope: discovery is limited to a client\nL145:    requesting and obtaining the minimum public discovery information for\nL146:    an AI resource (for example, its type, reachability information,\nL147: \nL148: \nL149: \nL150: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                   [Page 3]\nL151: \nL152: \nL153: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL154: \nL155:    communication protocol, and services) within a local network, within\nL156:    an organization, or between cooperating parties with trust\nL157:    relationships over the public Internet.  The charter assigns this\nL158:    information to two deliverables: the Discovery Architecture captures\nL159:    the requirements behind it, and the Protocol specifications, possibly\nL160:    one per deployment context, define it.  This document uses \"MDI\" as\nL161:    shorthand for the information the charter calls minimum public\nL162:    discovery information; where the two might differ, the charter's\nL163:    wording governs.\nL164:    Meanwhile, the gap is being filled independently and incompatibly by\nL165:    adjacent ecosystems: DNS-based bootstrap\nL166:    ([I-D.mozleywilliams-dnsop-dnsaid]), well-known JSON manifests, agent\nL167:    cards, and signed capability documents each carry a slightly\nL168:    different minimal field set with slightly different semantics, each\nL169:    bound to its own substrate.  A descriptor that is minimal and\nL170:    sufficient in one system is unreadable in another.\nL171:    [I-D.farrel-dawn-terminology] distinguishes the input of discovery,\nL172:    the Minimum Viable Dataset (MVD), from MDI, which it defines by what\nL173:    an entity provides to become discoverable and describes as what a\nL174:    lookup returns.  This document considers MDI at both points,\nL175:    publication and response, and relates it to the input.  Following the\nL176:    distinction [RFC3444] draws between information models and data\nL177:    models, it describes the information involved in discovery\nL178:    (Section 5), proposes how to decide what is minimal (Section 6),\nL179:    applies that procedure to the charter's three deployment contexts\nL180:    (Section 7), and describes the elements of the common discovery\nL181:    header and the minimal constraints that hold among them (Section 8),\nL182:    leaving encodings to bindings.  Section 13 lists the points on which\nL183:    input is sought.\nL184: 1.1.  Requirements Language\nL185: \nL186:    The key words \"MUST\", \"MUST NOT\", \"REQUIRED\", \"SHALL\", \"SHALL NOT\",\nL187:    \"SHOULD\", \"SHOULD NOT\", \"RECOMMENDED\", \"NOT RECOMMENDED\", \"MAY\", and\nL188:    \"OPTIONAL\" in this document are to be interpreted as described in\nL189:    BCP 14 [RFC2119] [RFC8174] when, and only when, they appear in all\nL190:    capitals, as shown here.\nL191: \nL192: 2.  Terminology\nL193:    This document uses the terms defined in\nL194:    [I-D.farrel-dawn-terminology], including AI Resource, Entity,\nL195:    Discovered Entity, Discovering Entity, Discovery, Discovery\nL196:    Information, Discovery Scope, Discoverable Object, Capability Card,\nL197:    Trust Indicator, and, centrally, Minimum Discoverable Information\nL198:    (MDI); readers are assumed to be familiar with that document.  The\nL199: Cui & Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                   [Page 4]\nL200: \nL201: \nL202: Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026\nL203: \nL204: \nL205:    elements defined here are the normalized slots through which a\nL206:    minimal subset of an entity's Properties is conveyed as Discovery\nL207:    Information.\nL208:    The entity that MDI describes is a Discovered Entity, which in DAWN\nL209:    is an AI system or AI resource [I-D.farrel-dawn-terminology]; the\nL210:    charter's AI resource \"may be an individual named agent or a service\nL211:    such as a catalog that will further the discovery process\"\nL212:    [DAWN-CHARTER].  In this document, \"resource\" is short for AI\nL213:    resource.  The client, as the charter calls it, is the Discovering\nL214:    Entity; this document also calls it the discovering party.  In\nL215:    addition:\nL216:    Discovery response:  What a discovery mechanism returns to a client;\nL217:       it may carry element values for one or more entities.\nL218: \nL219:    Known context:  Information the client already has when it interprets\nL220:       a discovery response, such as the Discovery Scope it searched, the\nL221:       name it started from, or the network it is attached to.  Some of\nL222:       it is part of the MVD (Section 5).\n"
